   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the village law, in relation to the population of a village",
    "basePrintNo": "S6595",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6595-2019",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §§2-200, 2-202 & 2-206, Vil L",
    "lawSection": "Village Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S6595\n\nSPONSOR: SKOUFIS\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the village law, in relation to the\npopulation of a village\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo raise the threshold for forming a new village from 500 residents to\n1,000 residents.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1: Amends section 2-200 of the Village Law to state that any\nterritory containing a population of at least one thousand persons may\nbe incorporated as a village under the provisions of the Village Law.\n\nSection 2: Amends section 2-202 of the Village Law to require a petition\nfor incorporation to include an allegation that the territory to be\nincorporated must contain a population of at least one thousand regular\ninhabitants.\n\nSection 3: Amends section 2-206 of the Village Law to allow an objection\nto be raised to a village incorporation petition at the town supervi-\nsor's hearing on the petition that the proposed territory does not\ncontain a population of at least one thousand regular inhabitants.\n\nSection 4: Effective date\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThe incorporation of new village is a major development for any town,\nand one that can have significant fiscal impacts on taxpayers throughout\nthe town (or towns) in which a new village is to be located.  Current\nlaw allows the incorporation of new villages with as few as 500 inhabit-\nants, a population threshold that has been unchanged for decades even as\nthe population of the state has grown and there has been an increased\nawareness of the costs to taxpayers that having a multiplicity of small\nvillages may cause. Very small villages of fewer than 1,000 residents\nmay have difficulty providing the services that residents rely on, and\nit can be difficult for villages of this size to find qualified resi-\ndents to serve on the village board and on local land use boards. In\norder to address these issues, this bill raises the minimum number of\nresidents to form a new village in New York from 500 regular inhabitants\nto 1,000 regular inhabitants.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNone\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone to the state.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately and shall apply to any petitions\nthat have not reached final disposition under the provision of article 2\nof the village law prior to the effective date of this act.\n",
